<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">January Uprising</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1863">1863</a>–<a href="/years/1864">1864</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The January Uprising was a Polish and Lithuanian insurrection against Russian rule, beginning in January 1863 in Congress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Fought mainly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> bands under a secret national government, it was crushed by 1864. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> responded with executions, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> and the abolition of the kingdom's remaining autonomy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/january-uprising.jpg" alt="January Uprising" width="320" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jan Matejko · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jan_Matejko_-_Polonia_1863_(Poland_Enchained)_-_MNK_XII-453_-_National_Museum_Krak%C3%B3w.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Despite the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>'s defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-ii-of-russia">Alexander II</a> warned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> in 1856 to forget any dreams. Two currents emerged in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a>. The Whites, liberal-conservative and mostly landowners, centred on Andrzej Zamoyski, hoped for an orderly return to the constitutional status before 1830. The Reds, a democratic movement of peasants, workers and some clergy, wanted the peasantry freed unconditionally. In February 1861 the Russian army attacked a demonstration in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, killing five, and on 8 April Russian fire killed 200 and wounded 500.</p><p>Aleksander Wielopolski, head of civil administration, brought forward to January 1863 the conscription of young Polish activists into the Imperial Russian Army for 20 years of service, hoping to derail the national movement. It sparked the uprising he had meant to avoid, and many young men fled to the forests.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The uprising began on 22 January 1863, organised by the clandestine Central National Committee and then by the Polish National Government. About 10,000 men rallied at first, mostly city workers and minor clerks, with younger sons of the poorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szlachta">szlachta</a> and lower priests. The insurgents fought as lightly armed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> bands that avoided large battles. On 1 February 1863 the uprising spread to the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>, and by spring it reached the Kiev Governorate. France, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> did not intervene militarily.</p><p>Disagreement between Whites and Reds weakened coordination, and the peasantry could be mobilised only where Polish units dominated. Romuald Traugutt took command on 17 October 1863. On 27 December 1863 he decreed that peasants would be granted the land they worked, but the policy came too late, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> had already begun offering peasants parcels of land. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-ii-of-russia">Alexander II</a> abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1864.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>According to official Russian information, 396 people were executed and 18,672 exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, and over 60,000 people were imprisoned and exiled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> to distant regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Whole villages and towns were burned. The authorities confiscated 1,660 estates in Poland and 1,794 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, ended the remaining autonomy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a>, and restricted the official use of Polish. Mikhail Muravyov, called the Hangman, supervised hangings, and Count Fyodor Berg pursued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russification">Russification</a>.</p><p>The uprising failed militarily but became a symbol of continuing resistance. In the 1860s and after, two movements grew out of it, the Polish Socialist Party under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-pilsudski">Józef Piłsudski</a> and the National Democracy movement under Roman Dmowski. Many turned to organic work and self-improvement.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “January Uprising” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1863 the <b>January Uprising</b> broke out, and Russia and Prussia signed the Alvensleben Convention on 8 February; the rising lasted two years before it was crushed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congress-poland">Congress Poland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The title once carried honour: Giuseppe Garibaldi officially styled himself “Dictator” of Sicily during the Expedition of the Thousand in 1860, and leaders of Poland’s November Uprising of 1830 and <b>January Uprising</b> of 1863 held the same title.</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Scholars think it was also influenced by the Polish patriotic song &quot;Poland Is Not Yet Lost&quot; (1797), popular among the peoples of the former Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth then struggling for independence; the <b>January Uprising</b> began a few months later.</blockquote><a href="/songs/national-anthem-of-ukraine">State Anthem of Ukraine</a></li><li><blockquote>The music is by Józef Pławiński, who was imprisoned with him, and draws partly on &quot;Marsz Żuawów&quot;, a song of the <b>January Uprising</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/warszawianka">Warszawianka (Whirlwinds of Danger)</a></li></ul></section>
